/// A two-argument collection method given one argument raises, catchably.
///
/// Issue #27.  Put, Set and Insert each take two arguments, and ALGOL-24.md's
/// 'Expected 2 arguments but got 1.' is the sentence for supplying one.
///
/// ⚠️ This is the worst of the three and the one that reads as harmless.  It
/// neither crashes nor refuses: 'collection_method' (bootstrap/algol.c:2345)
/// reads args[1] past the end of an argument array holding one element, and
/// then WRITES what it found into the collection.  Compiled, at 43207e9:
///
///     var M := [:];  M.Put (1);          -->  [1:]        Length 1
///     var L := [1,2,3];  L.Insert (0);   -->  [, 1, 2, 3] Length 4
///
/// The map gains a key whose value is whatever was in memory past the
/// arguments, and the list gains an element of the same provenance.  Both
/// render as though empty, which is what makes it quiet -- an indeterminate
/// value that prints as nothing is indistinguishable from an empty string until
/// something compares it.  A crash is louder and therefore safer than this.
///
/// ⚠️ Separate from ArityCollectionTooFew.a24 although both supply too few.
/// They fail by different mechanisms and a partial fix can close one without
/// the other: guarding args[0] against a NULL array stops the segfault and
/// leaves this untouched, because here args is a perfectly valid array that is
/// simply shorter than the method reads.  Only checking 'count' against what
/// each method needs closes both.
///
/// ⚠️ Do not assert what the corrupted collection contains.  It is
/// indeterminate by construction -- uninitialised memory -- so an assertion on
/// it would be a test that is entitled to give a different answer on a
/// different machine, a different compiler, or a different day.  What is
/// asserted is that the call never gets that far.
///
/// Interpreted: passes.
/// Compiled:    the assertion fails -- nothing was raised, so Caught is empty.
///
test 'A Two-Argument Collection Method Given One Raises';
begin
    var M := [:];
    var Caught := '';

    try
        M.Put (1);
    except
        on E : String do Caught := E;
    end

    AssertEqual ('Expected 2 arguments but got 1.', Caught);

    var L := [1, 2, 3];

    Caught := '';
    try
        L.Insert (0);
    except
        on E : String do Caught := E;
    end

    AssertEqual ('Expected 2 arguments but got 1.', Caught);
end
